The '''District of Castejón''' ({{lang-bl|Distrito de Castejón}}), was a {{W|federal district|confederally-administered district}} in [[Balisca]] which existed from 9 July 1836 until its consolidation on 11 October 1920. It's consolidation with surrounding communes within the [[Alpujarra]] followed after a long and bitter political and public relations campaign from the 1880s through the 1910s. The historical boundaries of the District are now occupied by [[Coruxón]], [[Ampurias]], [[Expansión]], [[Sástago]] and [[Hisbanya]], the so-called "core boroughs" of Castejón. This new administration became what is now known as the ''[[Castejón Metropolis]]'', or, ambiguously, ''Castejón Region''.

The city developed in the post-independence period as the crossroads of trade in the [[Northern Triangle]], with its advantageous location on [[Salto Bay]] and it's proximity to the [[Maroon Confederacy]], which the government was seeking to strengthen ties, especially following the [[Siege of Castejón]]. The 1836 Confederal Convention saw Castejón emerge as a "middle ground", and the ''de facto'' confederal capital. At the time, the city was facing a large influx of refugees displaced during the fighting of the [[Baliscan Civil War|civil war]]. Industrialization and {{W|direct action}} during the [[Anyos Federales|Reconstruction regime]] laid the groundwork for the expansion of Castejón into a {{W|compact city}} on a "{{W|complete streets|complete}} and {{W|human scale|humane}}" scale heavily influenced by the ideas of {{W|Ildefons Cerdà}}.  

